{{nihongo|'''Masaharu Ashikaga'''|足利 正治|Ashikaga Masaharu}} was a Sanese politician who served as the 13th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1925 to 1932. A dominant figure of the [[Go-Shōkō Democracy]] who served as prime minister for seven years—the longest tenure of any Sanese premier—Ashikaga's government oversaw the expansion of Sanese commercial interests in the [[Shendan]] region and the implementation of the Universal Manhood Suffrage Act. He resigned after the military's growing independence from civilian control made governance impossible, an act that effectively marked the end of party government in Okaiken.

== Early life ==
Masaharu Ashikaga was born on 3 February 1874 in [[Toyozawa]], the son of a prominent merchant family that had profited from the opening of the Sanese economy after the [[Saito Restoration]]. The Ashikaga family belonged to the commercial bourgeoisie rather than the samurai aristocracy, and young Masaharu grew up in an environment that valued entrepreneurship, pragmatism, and engagement with the wider world.

Ashikaga attended the prestigious Toyozawa Academy before winning a place at [[Okami Imperial University]], where he studied law and political economy. He was an outstanding student, known for his eloquence and his ability to synthesize complex arguments. After graduating in 1897, he briefly worked in his family's trading company before entering politics.

Ashikaga's early career in business gave him a deep understanding of the commercial dynamics of the [[Shendan]] region. The Ashikaga trading company had extensive operations in Shendan ports, and Masaharu had personally visited many of the region's commercial centers during his years in the family business. This experience would shape his foreign policy as prime minister.

== Political career ==
Ashikaga entered politics in 1900, joining the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]] through the party's Toyozawa prefectural branch. He was elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in 1902 and quickly established himself as one of the party's most effective parliamentary debaters.

Ashikaga rose through the Seiyūkai's ranks during the turbulent years of the Go-Shōkō Democracy. He served as Minister of Commerce from 1916 to 1918, where he oversaw the expansion of Sanese commercial interests in the Shendan, and as Minister of Finance from 1920 to 1923, where he managed the fiscal crisis that followed the Mihashi assassination.

By 1925, Ashikaga had emerged as the dominant figure within the Rikken Seiyūkai. His combination of commercial expertise, parliamentary skill, and personal charisma made him the natural choice to succeed [[Takanibu Kinoshita]] as prime minister. His appointment on 19 May 1925 marked the return of party government after the military interlude of the Kinoshita administration.

== Premiership (1925–1932) ==
Ashikaga's seven-year premiership was the longest in Okaiken's history and the high point of the Go-Shōkō Democracy. His government pursued an ambitious agenda of commercial expansion, democratic reform, and economic modernization, even as the military's growing independence increasingly undermined his authority.

=== Universal Manhood Suffrage Act ===
[[File:Ashikaga Universal Manhood Suffrage 1926.png|thumb|right|200px|The passage of the Universal Manhood Suffrage Act of 1926 expanded the electorate from 3 million to over 12 million]]
The Ashikaga government's most significant domestic achievement was the passage of the Universal Manhood Suffrage Act of 1926. Building on the electoral reforms of the Mihashi era, the act abolished all remaining property qualifications for voting and established universal male suffrage for all citizens over the age of 25. The reform expanded the electorate from approximately 3 million to over 12 million, fundamentally transforming the character of the Diet.

The Suffrage Act was fiercely opposed by the conservative establishment, including the [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]] and much of the bureaucracy. Ashikaga pushed the legislation through the Diet by forming a broad coalition of the Seiyūkai, the Kenseikai, and smaller progressive parties. The act's passage was widely celebrated as the crowning achievement of the Go-Shōkō Democracy.

=== Commercial expansion in the Shendan ===
Ashikaga's government oversaw a dramatic expansion of Sanese commercial interests in the [[Shendan]] region.
[[File:Ashikaga Shendan Commercial Expansion 1928.png|thumb|right|200px|Sanese merchant vessels in the Shendan, c. 1928, during the commercial expansion]]
Drawing on his personal experience in the trading business, Ashikaga implemented a series of policies designed to make Okaiken the dominant commercial power in the Shendan:

* The '''Shendan Trade Development Act of 1927''' provided subsidies and tax incentives for Sanese companies operating in Shendan markets. The act authorized the creation of the Shendan Development Bank, which provided low-interest loans to companies expanding their operations in the region.

* The '''Merchant Marine Expansion Act of 1928''' funded the construction of new cargo vessels and passenger ships designed for Shendan trade routes. By 1930, the Sanese merchant marine had become the largest in the Shendan, carrying over 40% of the region's seaborne trade.

* The '''Shendan Commercial Treaty Network (1926–1930)''' was a series of bilateral trade agreements that Ashikaga negotiated with Shendan states. These treaties reduced tariffs on Sanese goods, established preferential trading arrangements, and created a network of commercial relationships that reduced Okaiken's dependence on [[Illypnian]]-controlled trade lanes.

The commercial expansion brought enormous wealth to Okaiken's trading companies and industrial firms, but it also increased tensions with the Illypnian colonial powers—particularly Volisania and Breisland—which viewed the Sanese commercial offensive as a threat to their colonial interests.

=== Growing military independence ===
The central political conflict of Ashikaga's premiership was the military's growing independence from civilian control. Despite Ashikaga's best efforts, the Army and Navy increasingly operated beyond the authority of the cabinet and the Diet.

The [[Saito Constitution]]'s provisions on independent command ({{nihongo|tōsuiken|統帥権}}) provided the legal basis for military autonomy. The Army and Navy argued that their operational decisions—including troop deployments, strategy, and budget allocations—were beyond the Diet's authority. Ashikaga challenged this interpretation, arguing that the Constitution required the military to operate under the direction of the cabinet.

The conflict came to a head in 1931, when the Army independently authorized the deployment of additional troops to the Shendan region without consulting the prime minister or the cabinet.
[[File:Ashikaga Military Independence Crisis 1931.png|thumb|right|200px|The 1931 crisis when the Army independently deployed troops to the Shendan without consulting the cabinet]]
Ashikaga demanded that the Army withdraw the troops and submit its deployment plans for cabinet review. The Army Chief of Staff refused, citing the constitutional right of independent command.

Ashikaga attempted to resolve the crisis by appealing directly to the Emperor, but the Emperor—advised by the House of Officers—declined to intervene. The incident demonstrated that the prime minister had no effective mechanism for enforcing civilian control over the military.

=== Resignation ===
By early 1932, Ashikaga's government was paralyzed. The military's independence had made it impossible to govern: the Army and Navy ignored cabinet directives, the budget process had broken down, and the Diet was deadlocked between parties that supported the military and those that defended civilian control.

Ashikaga resigned on 24 May 1932, delivering a farewell address to the Diet in which he warned that "the independence of the military from civilian authority has made democratic governance impossible." His resignation was widely seen as the effective end of party government in Okaiken. [[Shishido Hayashi]] was appointed as acting prime minister, beginning the era of military-dominated government that would last until the end of the war.

=== Domestic policy beyond suffrage ===
The Ashikaga government's domestic agenda extended well beyond the Universal Manhood Suffrage Act. The government pursued a comprehensive program of economic modernization, including the National Railway Expansion Act of 1927, which authorized the construction of new rail lines connecting the major industrial centers, and the Electric Power Development Act of 1928, which established a national grid and promoted the construction of hydroelectric dams. These infrastructure investments were critical to the rapid industrial expansion of the late 1920s, and they laid the groundwork for the industrial capacity that would be mobilized during the war.

On fiscal policy, Ashikaga continued the deficit spending approach pioneered by Takahashi, using government borrowing to finance infrastructure investment and economic stimulus. The government's fiscal strategy was controversial: the national debt grew significantly during Ashikaga's tenure, and conservative critics warned that the borrowing was unsustainable. However, the resulting economic growth increased tax revenues sufficiently to service the debt, and the strategy was widely regarded as successful until the Depression of the early 1930s reversed the gains.

The government also introduced the Labor Standards Act of 1929, which established minimum working hours, rest periods, and safety standards for industrial workers. The Act was a significant expansion of the labor protections enacted under the Mihashi government, and it represented the last major piece of social legislation passed before the militarist era. The Act's provisions were enforced through a new Labor Inspection Bureau within the Home Ministry, though the Bureau's effectiveness was limited by the government's reluctance to antagonize the industrial conglomerates that supported the Seiyūkai.

=== Foreign policy beyond the Shendan ===
Beyond the Shendan commercial expansion, the Ashikaga government pursued several significant foreign policy initiatives. The government negotiated the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty]] of 1928, a comprehensive alliance and trade agreement with [[Zong]] that provided for mutual defense, economic cooperation, and the coordination of diplomatic positions in the Shendan. The Treaty was the cornerstone of Okaiken's Shendan strategy, and it would remain in force—though increasingly strained—until the end of the war.

The Ashikaga government also sought to manage the growing tensions with the Illypnian colonial powers through diplomatic engagement. The Shendan Neutrality Proposal of 1929, which called for the demilitarization of the Shendan and the establishment of a multilateral trade framework, was Ashikaga's most ambitious diplomatic initiative. The proposal was rejected by Volisania and Breisland, which viewed it as an attempt to undermine their colonial authority, but it demonstrated Okaiken's willingness to pursue a negotiated settlement of the Shendan disputes.

In the Shansian relationship, Ashikaga pursued a policy of cautious engagement. The Joint Fisheries Commission, which had been proposed under the Saionji government, was finally established in 1927, providing a mechanism for the management of the disputed northern maritime boundary. The Commission's work reduced tensions along the border, though it did not resolve the underlying territorial disputes. The government also resisted the Army's demands for a military buildup along the Shansian border, arguing that confrontation with Shansi would distract from the more important commercial expansion in the Shendan.

The Kwangju relationship continued to deepen under Ashikaga, with the government authorizing increased economic investment and the expansion of the military advisory mission. The Ashikaga government's Kwangju policy was characterized by what critics called "economic imperialism"—the use of trade agreements and investment to establish de facto control over the Kwangju economy without the formal assumption of political authority. This approach was commercially successful but politically destabilizing, as it fueled nationalist sentiment in Kwangju and heightened tensions with Shansi.

== Legacy ==
Masaharu Ashikaga is remembered as the last great prime minister of the Go-Shōkō Democracy and the most important democratic reformer after [[Kenzan Mihashi]]. His Universal Manhood Suffrage Act permanently expanded the franchise and laid the groundwork for the democratic institutions that would eventually be restored after the war. His commercial expansion in the Shendan established Okaiken as the dominant economic power in the region—a position it would maintain for decades.

However, Ashikaga's legacy is also one of failure. Despite his seven years in office and his considerable political skills, he was unable to prevent the military's growing independence from civilian control. His resignation, and the circumstances that prompted it, demonstrated the fundamental weakness of the Saito constitutional order: a constitution that placed the military beyond civilian authority was incompatible with democratic governance.

After his resignation, Ashikaga retired from active politics. He lived to see the end of the war and the beginning of the post-war reconstruction, dying in [[Toyozawa]] on 12 November 1946 at the age of 72.

